WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:03.600
You can find home remedies
for all sorts of ailments,

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:07.000
but the science doesn’t
always back them up.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:07.760
What does it have to say

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:11.957
about using a common
household product on warts?

00:00:12.213 --> 00:00:18.723
"Removing Warts with Duct Tape"

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.800
When I was reviewing the science behind
common over-the-counter remedies

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:27.720
used in dermatology such as tea
tree oil for acne or nail fungus,

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:32.720
I was surprised to see on the same
page a section on duct tape.

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:34.200
Duct tape?!

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:37.320
The only time I remember seeing
duct tape used in a medical study

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:42.440
was on the identification of the gases
responsible for the odor of human farts,

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:46.320
which involved a collection system
comprised of gas-tight pantaloons

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:49.120
sealed to the skin with duct tape.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:52.200
That's the study where they
assessed the wind breaking ability

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:55.640
of a cushion called
the Toot Trapper.

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:59.920
But what the dermatology journal
was talking about is warts.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:04.040
Duct tape brings out our
inventive, slightly kooky side.

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:07.280
Given this versatility, it wasn't
so surprising a few years ago

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:12.840
when a group of doctors reported
that duct tape could get rid of warts.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:16.240
As I noted in my last wart video,
all sorts of strange things

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:20.280
are purported to cure warts
because most go away on their own.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:21.800
A thousand kids were
followed for two years

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:25.360
and two-thirds of the warts
disappeared without doing a thing.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:27.280
So maybe we should
just leave them alone,

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:30.080
although there are cases
that may warrant treatment.

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:33.520
Otherwise we can just let our
own body take care of them.

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:37.560
Warts are caused by wart viruses, and
so spontaneous wart disappearance

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:40.600
is assumed to be an immune response
where our body finally wakes up

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:43.040
and takes notice.
This assumption

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:47.080
is based on studies like this—where
foreign proteins were injected into

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:52.680
the wart itself, like a measles-mumps-
rubella vaccine straight into the wart—

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:57.360
which compared to placebo, appeared to
accelerate the immune clearance process.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:01.720
The problem, of course, is that
injections hurt, and 30% of the kids

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:05.240
that got their warts injected with the
vaccine suffered a flu-like syndrome.

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:09.080
Yikes! OK, scratch that.
What else do we got?

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:13.880
Within a few months any placebo treatment
will work in about a quarter of the cases.

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:18.440
So if you put duct tape on 100 warts and
23 go away, that wouldn't mean much.

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:22.680
The traditional medical therapies, acid
treatments and freezing treatments,

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:25.560
bump the cure rate up to about 50%.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:29.440
So if you were really serious about
testing the efficacy of duct tape,

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:32.360
you would pit it head to head
against one of those,

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:34.000
and that's exactly what happened.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:40.280
The efficacy of duct tape vs cryotherapy
in the treatment of the common wart.

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:44.520
Cryotherapy is one of the treatments
of choice for many pediatricians.

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:48.000
Objective: To determine if application
of duct tape is as effective

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.080
as cryotherapy in the treatment
of common warts.

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:54.320
Patients were randomized to receive
either liquid nitrogen applied

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:57.400
to each wart or
duct tape occlusion.

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:00.360
When I heard about treating
warts with duct tape,

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:02.680
I had this image where they were like
trying to rip them off or something.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:07.960
No, no, no, no. They're just applying a
little circle of duct tape every week or so.

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:14.400
Here are the details...

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:17.880
Although there had been a few
anecdotal reports of using tape,

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:22.600
no prospective, randomized controlled
trial had yet been performed until

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:28.720
this study, which found that the duct
tape was not only equal to but exceeded

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:32.200
the efficacy of cryotherapy, which
worked in 60% of the cases,

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:36.680
but 85% of the duct tape
patients were cured—significantly

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:40.120
more effective than cryotherapy
for treatment of the common wart.

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:44.360
More effective and fewer side effects.
The only adverse effect observed

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:47.000
in the duct tape group during
our study was a small,

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:54.360
minimal amount of local irritation and
redness, whereas cryotherapy hurts.

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:55.920
You want to hear the saddest thing?

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:01.760
One young child actually vomited in fear
of pain before each cryotherapy session.

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:04.760
They were like torturing the poor kid.

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:08.720
Cryotherapy can cause pain and
bloody blisters that can get infected

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:12.640
and mess up your nail bed.

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:19.160
So, duct tape: more effective, fewer
side effects and more convenient.

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:23.200
Compare applying a little duct tape at
home to making multiple clinic visits,

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:25.680
dragging the poor kid
back every two weeks.

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:27.800
I mean it's like a win-win-win.

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:30.640
Duct tape can now be offered
as a nonthreatening, painless,

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:34.360
and inexpensive technique for
the treatment of warts in children.

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:37.960
I mean how much does a
little piece of duct tape cost?

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:40.600
It's like a win-win-win-win.

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:45.760
Ah, but the money you save
is the money the doctor loses.

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:50.080
There's no way the medical profession is
going to just let this go unchallenged.

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:54.960
Further studies were performed
and failed to show an effect,

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:57.760
and so we end up in the medical
literature with conclusions like this:

00:04:57.760 --> 00:04:59.960
"Is duct tape effective
for treating warts?"

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:04.720
Bottom line?
No. Huh.

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:07.880
Is duct tape really not
effective after all,

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:13.400
or was there some kind of critical
design flaw in the follow-up studies?

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:18.480
We'll find out next.

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:22.160
One of the unusual, innovative,
and long-forgotten remedies noted

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:27.520
in this dermatology journal was
the use of duct tape to cure warts,

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:29.720
finally put to the test.

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:31.320
There's all sorts of
conventional therapies

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:34.680
from acid to cryosurgery to
lasers, but most of these

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:37.040
are expensive, or painful, or both.

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:39.960
Whereas the simple application
of duct tape is neither—

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:45.240
and may even be more effective
than trying to freeze the warts off.

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:48.360
Some doctors lauded the study,
noting they had been using

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:52.000
duct tape for decades as a
painless yet effective treatment,

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:55.200
as opposed to surgery and
other destructive therapies.

00:05:55.200 --> 00:06:00.200
The only downside being your patients
may think you're off your rocker,

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:06.440
but hey, when it works, they appreciate
your wise choice to minimize discomfort.

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:10.160
Other doctors were not so amused.
Here they are spending money

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:13.720
on all this fancy equipment
and then along comes duct tape?

00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:18.720
Studies like that "could damage
the reputation of cryosurgery,"

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:22.240
complaining that the 10-second
application of liquid nitrogen

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:26.320
they used in the study was too short,
so it was an unfair comparison.

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:28.800
And evidently you really have
to get in there and freeze

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:32.960
until they get a blistering reaction.
And yes, it hurts but too bad,

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:37.120
even making light of the poor child
who vomited out of fear of the pain

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:41.440
prior to each freezing by
making some joke about it.

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:45.960
Did they have a point, though, about
the 10 seconds? Well, in the duct tape

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:49.360
versus cryotherapy trial those
10-second cryotherapy treatments

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:53.280
worked 60% of the time, which
is actually better than the results

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:57.040
of most cryotherapy studies that
only seemed to cure about 50%.

00:06:57.040 --> 00:07:01.640
In fact, the typical cryotherapy
for warts works so poorly that

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:05.680
statistically it didn't even beat out
placebo, so all that pain may be

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:10.600
for nothing, though aggressive
cryotherapy does seem to work better.

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:14.040
See, what they should have done,
wrote another doctor in response

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:18.400
to the duct tape trial, is take a scalpel
to it and then really blister it up,

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:21.280
and then you go back a week later and
try to cut and crater it out, and then

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:27.080
maybe go back a third time, bragging that
they can get closer to a 90% cure rate.

00:07:27.080 --> 00:07:29.400
Yeah, but at what cost?

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:33.480
In the duct tape trial one
patient lost his study wart

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:36.480
in a trampoline toe-amputation accident.

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:41.480
Hey, there's a treatment that works,
100% effective: amputation!

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:44.920
But at what cost?

00:07:44.920 --> 00:07:48.640
Eighty-five percent of the duct tape
patients were cured with no pain

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:52.400
or tissue damage, whereas aggressive
cryotherapy may require lidocaine

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:56.720
injection, nerve blocks, to take
the "cry out of cryotherapy"

00:07:56.720 --> 00:07:59.120
and can cause permanent tissue damage.

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:03.360
You can end up with these
big necrotic frostbite lesions.

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:06.560
I mean in one sense tissue damage is
the whole point of freezing warts,

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:10.720
but you can end up causing these deep
burns or end up rupturing tendons,

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:15.760
which can cause permanent disability or
cause extensive scarring in rare cases.

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:18.840
Just the psychological stress
of having to keep going back

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:22.160
for this painful procedure may
ironically impair our ability

00:08:22.160 --> 00:08:26.000
to fight off the wart viruses
in the first place.

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:29.960
And so even if the effectiveness of duct
tape is shown to be merely equivalent

00:08:29.960 --> 00:08:33.280
to that of cryotherapy, it would
be better; and it was shown to be

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:36.720
even more effective,
in fact maybe most effective.

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:40.560
Compared to 10 other wart treatments,
duct tape beat out them all

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:43.440
in terms of effectiveness,
and also in terms of cost—

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:48.480
cheaper than all but the DN option,
which stands for Do Nothing.

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:51.560
Compared to the most cost-effective
prescription treatments available,

00:08:51.560 --> 00:08:56.480
OTC duct tape, meaning over-the-counter
duct tape, is 10 times cheaper.

00:08:56.480 --> 00:09:00.720
It's an unusual and welcome event in
health care when a common ailment

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:03.840
is proven equally amenable
to an inexpensive, tolerable,

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:08.600
and safe alternative therapy.
But wait.

00:09:08.600 --> 00:09:11.240
If you look at the latest
Cochrane review, which is like

00:09:11.240 --> 00:09:15.240
the gold-standard of evidence-based
reviews, they acknowledge that

00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:18.160
cryotherapy is less convenient,
more painful, more expensive.

00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:22.240
But while in the earlier review they
did get excited about the effectiveness

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:27.720
of duct tape, in two subsequent studies
duct tape seemed to totally flop.

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:29.960
So should we give up
on duct tape for warts

00:09:29.960 --> 00:09:33.520
or is there another side of the story?

00:09:33.520 --> 00:09:37.963
We'll find out in the
thrilling conclusion next.

